.TH FSTRACE 1 "2026-07-06" "fstrace 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fstrace \- trace filesystem accesses of a command using eBPF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fstrace
.RI [ -- ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.br
.B fstrace
.RB [ -h | --help ]
.RB [ -V | --version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fstrace
runs
.I COMMAND
and reports every filesystem path it accesses, using eBPF to observe the
relevant system calls.
.PP
It is the unprivileged client of a two-part design. All privileged work \(em
loading and attaching the eBPF programs \(em is performed once by the
.BR fstrace-daemon (1)
background process, so
.B fstrace
itself needs no
.BR sudo .
The client forks and execs
.IR COMMAND ,
asks the daemon to trace it, and turns the kernel events streamed back over a
Unix socket into resolved, filtered reports. A running
.BR fstrace-daemon (1)
is therefore required.
.PP
Everything from
.I COMMAND
onward \(em including options such as
.B \-\-help
\(em is passed through to the traced program unchanged. For example,
.B fstrace ls \-\-help
runs
.B ls \-\-help
under trace.
.SH OPTIONS
These options are recognised only when they appear
.I before
.IR COMMAND .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End
.BR fstrace 's
own options; treat everything that follows as
.IR COMMAND .
.SH REPORTS
File-access reports are written to
.BR "file descriptor 3" ,
one per line, in the form:
.PP
.RS
.I <access_type><file_type>
.I <file_path>
.RE
.PP
.I access_type
is
.B R
(read),
.B W
(write/create),
.B D
(delete) or
.B E
(enumerate);
.I file_type
is
.B F
(file),
.B D
(directory) or
.B X
(does not exist).
.PP
Redirect fd 3 to capture reports, e.g.
.B fstrace ls 3>&1
(to standard output) or
.B fstrace ls 3>reports.txt
(to a file). Alternatively set
.B FSTRACE_REPORT_FILE
to a path; this is recommended under
.BR sudo ,
which closes inherited descriptors \(>= 3. If fd 3 is not open and
.B FSTRACE_REPORT_FILE
is unset,
.B fstrace
exits with an error explaining how to fix the invocation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Paths in the filter variables are colon\-separated. The filter decision is:
ignore if a negative filter matches; otherwise allow if a positive filter
matches; otherwise ignore.
.TP
.B FSTRACE_FILTER_PREFIX
Only report paths starting with one of these prefixes (default
.BR / ).
.TP
.B FSTRACE_FILTER_SUBSTRING
Only report paths containing one of these substrings.
.TP
.B FSTRACE_NEGATIVE_FILTER_PREFIX
Ignore paths starting with one of these prefixes.
.TP
.B FSTRACE_NEGATIVE_FILTER_SUBSTRING
Ignore paths containing one of these substrings.
.TP
.B FSTRACE_DEBOUNCE
Set to
.B 1
to debounce duplicate reports. Sending
.B SIGUSR1
flushes the debounce cache.
.TP
.B FSTRACE_REPORT_FILE
Write the report stream to this path instead of file descriptor 3.
.TP
.B FSTRACE_SOCKET
Path to the daemon's Unix socket (default
.BR /run/fstrace/fstrace.sock ).
Must match the daemon.
.TP
.BR FSTRACE_LOG_FILE ", " FSTRACE_LOG_DIR
Write log output to an explicit file, or to a per\-process file under a
directory (\c
.IR fstrace- <pid>\c
.BR .log ),
respectively.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log verbosity using
.BR tracing / env\-filter
syntax, e.g.
.BR fstrace=trace .
.B FSTRACE_DEBUG=1
is shorthand for
.BR fstrace=debug .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B fstrace
propagates the exit status of
.IR COMMAND :
a normal exit code is returned as\-is, and termination by signal
.I N
is reported as
.RB 128+ N
(e.g. 143 for
.BR SIGTERM ).
It exits
.B 1
on its own startup errors and
.B 127
if
.I COMMAND
cannot be executed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Trace a command, sending reports to standard output:
.PP
.RS
.B fstrace touch /tmp/foo 3>&1
.RE
.PP
Trace only accesses under
.BR /tmp ,
writing reports to a file:
.PP
.RS
.B FSTRACE_FILTER_PREFIX=/tmp fstrace make 3>reports.txt
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fstrace-daemon (1)
.PP
Project documentation:
.UR https://github.com/bmiddha/fstrace
.UE
.SH AUTHOR
Bharat Middha and contributors.
